{{Quote|Do you have any idea what this is? This is an N-1 starfighter, handmade for the royal guard and commissioned personally by the Queen of Naboo.|Peli Motto|<ref name="Mando Boba" />}}
The '''N-1 starfighter''', also referred to as the '''Royal Naboo N-1 starfighter''', '''Naboo N-1 starfighter''', '''Naboo starfighter''', '''N-1 fighter''', or more simply, the '''N-1''',<ref name="Complete Vehicles" /> was a single seat patrol [[Starfighter|craft]] developed by the [[Theed Palace Space Vessel Engineering Corps]] and used by the [[Royal Naboo Security Forces]].<ref name="SW EOSAOV (SE)" /><ref name="USW NE" /><ref name="Complete Vehicles" />

==Characteristics==
{{Quote|The Jawas had a turbonic venturi assimilator from a Galactic Republic-era starfighter?|Din Djarin, to Peli Motto|<ref name="Mando Boba" />}}
[[File:N-1_Starfighter.png|thumb|left|220px|The N1 starfighter was a nimble craft.]]
The N-1 [[starfighter]], was a sleek single-[[pilot]] craft used by [[Naboo (people)|the Naboo]] and demonstrated the marriage of art and function present in [[Naboo]] technology. Buffed [[chromium]] finishes on the craft's forward surfaces glinted in the sun for fly-bys and [[parade]]s, and represented the royalty of Naboo, complementing the [[Naboo Royal Starship|Royal Starship]].<ref name="Complete Vehicles" /> The N-1's chromium stood in stark contrast to its overall [[Color|yellow]] finish.<ref name="Ships of the Galaxy" />

The craft carried two [[J-type engine|J-type]] [[engine]]s, which rendered the starfighter an agile craft, and a Nubian [[Monarc C-4 hyperdrive]] engine, and had a single on-board [[astromech droid]] which was mounted on the top of the craft. It featured an [[autopilot]] system and shielding, along with offensive weaponry that consisted of two [[blaster cannon]]s and integrated [[proton torpedo launcher]]s, with one double proton torpedo magazine, that could carry ten proton torpedoes. It could attain a maximum speed of 1,100 [[Kilometer per hour|kph]] and was eleven [[meter]]s in length. The ship's central rat-tail acted as a power charger collector, and received energy from onboard generators when it was not in use. The ships two outer finials served as heat sinks for the engines.<ref name="Ships of the Galaxy" /><ref name="DB" />

==History==
===Republic Era===
{{Quote|Now THIS is podracing!|Anakin Skywalker|audio=NowThisIsPodracing.flac|<ref name="TPM" />}}
[[File:Space_Battle_of_Naboo.png|thumb|left|240px|N-1s attack the Trade Federation's ''Lucrehulk''-class droid control ship at the Battle of Naboo]]
In use since at least the [[Standard day|days]] of the [[High Republic Era]],<ref name="Ecilpse Highlights">{{SW|url=news/star-wars-eclipse-cinematic-reveal-trailer|text=5 Highlights from the ''Star Wars Eclipse'' Cinematic Reveal Trailer}}</ref> the N-1 starfighter was originally built by the Naboo,<ref name="DB" /> and was most notably used during the [[Trade Federation]] [[Invasion of Naboo|invasion]] of the [[planet]].<ref name="TPM" /> Few of the pilots in [[Naboo Royal Space Fighter Corps|Naboo's Space Fighter Corps]] had actual combat experience, but their rigorous training program prepared them for the battle against the Trade Federation.<ref name="SWEncyclopedia">''[[Star Wars Encyclopedia: The Comprehensive Guide to the Star Wars Galaxy]]''</ref> During the invasion, the pilots of [[Bravo Squadron]] flew the N-1 during the final assault against the [[Lucrehulk-class battleship|Droid Control Ship]] ''[[Vuutun Palaa]]''. Young [[Anakin Skywalker]] fired a pair of torpedoes into its starboard [[Reactor|main reactor]], effectively destroying it from within. They later joined in the celebration on the planet below, flying overhead Naboo's [[capital]] of [[Theed]].<ref name="TPM" />

A [[Standard decade|decade]] later, three N-1s were used to escort [[Senator]] [[Padmé Amidala Naberrie|Padmé Amidala]] to [[Coruscant]] in order to vote on the [[Military Creation Act]]. Their Royal [[Starship]] held a decoy senator, and Amidala flew in one of the starfighters. They landed without incident, but the bounty hunter [[Zam Wesell]] hit the ship shortly after, [[Plot to assassinate Senator Amidala|destroying]] it and [[Death|killing]] the senator's [[Cordé|double]]; one of the three N-1s was also knocked off the landing platform by flying debris.<ref name="AOTC">{{Film|II}}</ref> N-1s remained in Naboo service during the [[Clone Wars]], with several being kept in [[Theed Hangar]] during this time.<ref name="Blue Shadow Virus">{{TCW|Blue Shadow Virus (episode)}}</ref> During the war, [[Jedi]] [[Padawan]] [[Ahsoka Tano]] trained Amidala in [[starfighter combat]], with Amidala flying an [[Padmé Amidala's N-1 starfighter|all-chrome N-1]].<ref name="The Starfighter Stunt">{{FOD|The Starfighter Stunt}}</ref>

===New Republic===
[[File:DinDjarinN1-BHUSC.png|thumb|right|250px|Din Djarin's highly modified N-1 starfighter.]]
[[Standard year|Years]] later, following the climactic [[Battle of Endor]], N-1 starfighters flew in celebration after the [[Galactic Empire]]'s [[DS-2 Death Star II Mobile Battle Station|second Death Star]] was destroyed.<ref name="ROTJ">{{Film|VI}} ([[The Star Wars Trilogy Special Edition|Special Edition]])</ref> Shortly thereafter, Imperial [[agent]]s received posthumous orders from [[Darth Sidious|Emperor Palpatine]] to commence the destruction of Naboo via weather manipulation as part of [[Operation: Cinder]]. [[Alliance to Restore the Republic|Rebel]] leader [[Leia Skywalker Organa Solo|Leia Organa]], ace pilot [[Shara Bey]], and Naboo's own [[Sosha Soruna|Queen Soruna]] flew three long-abandoned N-1 fighters in the Theed Hangar into [[Realspace|space]] to [[Battle of Theed|destroy]] the Imperial [[satellite]]s that were causing the extreme weather conditions.<ref name="Shattered Empire 3">[[Shattered Empire 3|''Shattered Empire'' 3]]</ref>

The Mandalorian bounty hunter [[Din Djarin]] acquired a [[Din Djarin's N-1 starfighter|modified N-1 starfighter]] following the destruction of his previous starship, the ''[[Razor Crest]]''. Djarin and the [[engineer]] [[Peli Motto]] constructed the ship at [[Hangar 3-5]] in [[Mos Eisley]], [[Tatooine]].<ref name="Mando Boba" />

[[File:Leema_Kais_N-1_starfighter-BFII.jpg|thumb|left|254px|Leema Kai's N-1 Starfighter]]
By the [[time]] of the [[Cold War]], an [[Leema Kai's N-1 starfighter|N-1 starfighter]] was in the service of [[Jinata Security]] [[officer]] [[Leema Kai]], and was modified with [[seismic charge]]s in place of proton torpedoes. It was destroyed in a [[Mission to Athulla|dogfight]] with [[Iden Versio]].<ref name="Star Wars Battlefront 2" />

In [[35 ABY]],<ref name="TROS Date">''[[Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker: The Visual Dictionary]]'' dates the events of [[Star Wars: Episode IX The Rise of Skywalker|''Star Wars'': Episode IX ''The Rise of Skywalker'']] to one [[Standard year|year]] after the events of [[Star Wars: Episode VII The Force Awakens|''Star Wars'': Episode VII ''The Force Awakens'']]. As ''[[Star Wars: Galactic Atlas]]'' dates the events of ''The Force Awakens'' to [[34 ABY]], the events of ''The Rise of Skywalker'' must occur in [[35 ABY]].</ref> N-1 starfighters were part of a [[Citizens' Fleet|fleet]] assembled by [[Landonis Balthazar Calrissian|Lando Calrissian]] to assist the Resistance during the [[Battle of Exegol|battle]] against the [[Sith Eternal military|Sith Eternal forces]] over the planet [[Exegol]].<ref name="Episode IX">{{Film|IX}}</ref>

==Behind the scenes==
{{Quote|And that one, I honestly wasn't sure that it would work, because it was kind of a risky design to begin with. But at the end, it fit all the requirements. It was iconic, it was simple to read, you could tell what it was, and it was fresh. I was very happy that George [Lucas] actually liked it and actually wanted to push it even further by making it a bold color.|Doug Chiang, referring to the N-1 starfighter|<ref name="Oral history">{{SW|url=news/star-wars-episode-i-the-phantom-menace-oral-history|text="All Films Are Personal": An Oral History of ''Star Wars'': Episode I ''The Phantom Menace''}}</ref>}}
The N-1 starfighter first appeared in the [[1999]] film [[Star Wars: Episode I The Phantom Menace|''Star Wars'': Episode I ''The Phantom Menace'']], the first installment of the ''[[Star Wars]]'' [[prequel trilogy]].<ref name="TPM" /> A few practical models of the ship were built for the prequel trilogy. N-1 starfighters were later added to [[Star Wars: Episode VI Return of the Jedi|''Star Wars'': Episode VI ''Return of the Jedi'']] as part of the [[Rally on Naboo|celebration]] on Naboo in the ending montage in the [[Star Wars Trilogy (DVD)|DVD]] release.<ref name="Ships of the Galaxy Article">{{SW|url=news/ships-of-the-galaxy-naboo-n-1-starfighter|text=Ships of the Galaxy: Naboo N-1 Starfighter|int=Ships of the Galaxy}}</ref>

The N-1 starfighter was designed by artist [[Doug Chiang]], and it was one of his favorite designs.<ref name="Oral history" /> At the start of the design phase, the ship had the angular design aesthetic prevalent in the [[original trilogy]].<ref name="Old DB">{{DB|starship|naboon1starfighter|Naboo N-1 Starfighter}}</ref> However, Chiang decided that Naboo's ships should have an aquatic feel because Naboo had a lot of [[water]]. Chiang thought that the ship would land on water, so he designed the ship to have a [[boat]]-like hull bottom and not have landing pads. He loved the idea of a really sleek boat form. Chiang knew that [[George Lucas]] loved F1 cars and F1 boats, and he found the F1 cars' lines to be striking. Chiang decided to turn the look of an F1 boat into a ship, which started the evolution of the ships's design.<ref name="Oral history" />

Chiang knew that the ship would need to be functionally practical and also have the elegant flavor that Lucas wanted. In the design, Chiang turned the F1 boats' two pontoons into jet engines. Chiang designed the engines with a really long spike to mirror a [[Wikipedia:Fish|fish's]] tail spike in order to give the ship a fishlike quality. Chiang massaged all of the details to fully complete the ship.<ref name="Oral history" />

Chiang wasn't sure that the ship would make it into ''The Phantom Menace'' because it had a risky design. The ship ultimately made it into the film because it was iconic, simple to read, fresh, and the audience could tell what it was. Lucas liked the design and wanted to go further by giving the ship a bold [[color]].<ref name="Oral history" />

During production of ''The Phantom Menace'', Visual Effects Supervisor [[John Knoll]] and the film's crew ran into a problem because the N-1 starfighters' tails went through the walls of the [[Theed Hangar/Legends|Theed hangar]] in order to fit into their stalls. During production of the ''[[Star Wars: The Clone Wars]]'' episode "[[Blue Shadow Virus (episode)|Blue Shadow Virus]]," the show's crew dealt with the same problem. Supervising director [[Dave Filoni]] consulted with Knoll, who confirmed they also had this problem while working on ''The Phantom Menace''.<ref name="Blue Shadow Virus Ep Guide">{{SW|url=series/clone-wars/blue-shadow-virus-episode-guide|text="Blue Shadow Virus" Episode Guide &#124; The Clone Wars}}</ref>

The full-size N-1 Starfighter prop used by Anakin Skywalker in [[Star Wars: Episode I The Phantom Menace|''Star Wars'': Episode I ''The Phantom Menace'']], was on display in Boston's [[Wikipedia:Museum of Science, Boston|Museum of Science]] as part of the [[Star Wars: Where Science Meets Imagination]] exhibit in 2005, and remained on display in the museum's Blue Wing for several years afterward, hanging above guest's heads with several large wires. It was ultimately taken down several years later, having accumulated large amounts of dust in the process.<ref name="SMI">''[[Star Wars: Where Science Meets Imagination]]''</ref>

